titre $ Key to the mycenoid species - exclusive of the Mycena species- Gro Gulden
com$ Gro Gulden A preliminary guide to the macromycetes in the Finse area / GF 2011
1,10$Mycenoid genera
1,2 $ Lamellae ascending, spores (sub)globose, smooth or nodulose, with prominent apiculus
1,5 $ Lamellae decurrent, spores different (ellipsoid, ellipsoid-fusoid (towards apiculus), or bluntly fusoid-citriniform)
2,0 $ Spores nodulose, cystidia branched at apex $ Mycenella margaritispora
2,3 $ Spores smooth, cystidia ventricose and apically  fusoid to (sub)capitate
3,4 $ Basidia 4-spored
3,0 $ Basidia 2-spored $ Mycenella salicina var. b
4,0 $ Spores > 6.8 µm, cystidia ca 35-70 x 7-11µm $ Mycenella salicina
4,0 $ Spores < 6.8 µm, cystidia ca 20-38 x 4-6 µm $ Mycenella salicina var. a
5,0 $ Pileus pale yellow- to pinkish brown, spores fusoid-citriform $ Hemimycena ochrogaleata var.' paucicystidiata'
5,6 $ Pileus white or with pale grey centre and radial striae, spores ellipsoid to ellipsoid-fusoid
6,0 $ Lamellae normal, smell nitrous, pileus with pale grey 'eye' when moist, caulocystidia with rounded apex $ Hemimycena delectabilis var. paucicystidiata
6,0 $ Lamellae ± reduced, few, smell indistinct, caulocystidia attenuated, ± setae-like $ Hemimycena pseudocrispula
10,0$A total of 17 taxa of Mycena and related species from the Finse area were treated by Gulden & Jenssen (1982) – in a paper presented for the first ISAM (1980). Since then many more collections have been made and at present 28 species in the genera Hemimycena (3) Mycena (23) and Mycenella (2) are known in the area.///The species of Rickenella are treated in the omphalinoid group. ///Included genera: Hemimycena, Mycena and Mycenella.
